          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "cmux could not hand this command to its bundled command-line tool because forwarding resolved back to the app itself. Reinstall cmux or run the command from a standard cmux CLI installation."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.forwarding.error.missingBundledCLI": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "cmux could not run this command from the app bundle. Reinstall cmux or run the command from a standard cmux CLI installation."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.browser": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Use browsers and computer use\n\nFor browser automation on the machine, run `agent-browser` there and read its documentation. Prefer the machine's existing headful Chrome session when it exposes DevTools. The devbox installs Chrome, but its dock launcher does not enable remote debugging by default. If no debugging-enabled session exists, use a machine terminal under the work-user desktop session to start a separate visible Chrome with `DISPLAY=:1`, `--remote-debugging-port=9222`, and a dedicated `--user-data-dir`, then use `agent-browser --cdp 9222 snapshot`, or try `agent-browser --auto-connect snapshot`. `cloud exec` is for short provider commands and may not have the work-user desktop bus. Inspect the installed browser and driver with `command -v` and `--version`; `cloud tools` only checks general utilities. The default devbox also installs `cua-driver` for Linux desktop automation. Run `cua-driver doctor` from the machine terminal and read its X11 and AT-SPI results; a zero exit alone does not prove the desktop is ready. Use `cua-driver list-tools` to inspect its actions. Configure a remote agent's MCP host to launch `cua-driver mcp`; use `cua-driver call <tool> [JSON args]` for shell automation. These commands need the desktop display (`DISPLAY=:1`) and accessibility bus. Use `cua-driver` for visible desktop windows and `agent-browser` for web pages. Historical shell-only machines may not have a desktop or driver. Inspect the remote screen, act, then verify. Local computer-use tools target your Mac unless configured for a remote target."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.choose": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Choose a machine\n\nInspect existing machines first. Route the current directory with `route --json`; reuse its machine when `would_provision` is false. When `machine` is null and `would_provision` is true, check `ls` and choose Base, an idle machine, or deliberate provisioning before creating anything. Check `vpn status`; run `vpn up` if a private operation reports a down or stale tunnel. Base is a persistent machine with a workspace that you can reopen. Prefer a new workspace on an existing machine; use `new` for isolation. Use returned machine, workspace, and terminal IDs in later commands."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.intro": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "cmux Cloud runs commands, agents, browsers, and desktops on remote machines. Run the commands below from cmux on your Mac. Cloud operations need the app and a signed-in account. `cmux cloud --skill` prints this same guide; `cmux vm` is an alias for `cmux cloud`."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.more": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Find exact methods\n\nRead command help for flags and examples. The full Cloud reference covers machine lifecycle, workspaces, terminals, and transfers. Use `cmux guide` for local workspace, browser, and computer-use methods."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.share": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Observe, open, share, and clean up\n\nUse `tree`, `terminal read`, and `terminal wait` to observe live work and check its real output. Use `open` for a machine's terminal, desktop, or web port when there is something useful to show. Private web URLs need the Cloud network connection; read `vpn` help. Use `domains` to publish a web port with explicit access rules. Use snapshots and forks to keep or copy machine state. Delete forks and scratch machines you created. Never remove or reset a machine you did not create."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.cloud.work": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Run work and collect results\n\nUse `exec` for a short non-GUI command on a chosen machine. Use `run -- <command>` to route a bounded command; add `--sync` only when you want to upload the current directory. Use `dev <machine> --no-open` to stage a recognized project with its development layout, or use `agent` to start a coding agent. Use a machine `terminal` for long-running, interactive, or GUI work. Machine workspaces and terminals keep running when a pane closes or the Mac sleeps; terminal IDs come from `tree`, and you can reattach with the `vm open <machine>/<workspace>/<terminal>` address. Use `push` and `pull` to transfer files. Use `terminal wait` for a screen pattern and `terminal wait-exit` plus `terminal output` for process completion and logs. Read terminal output before reporting success."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.description": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Read a short guide and find specific methods. No running app, network access, or sign-in is needed to read it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.browser": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Use a browser\n\nUse `cmux browser` for a browser surface inside cmux. Use `agent-browser` for a separate browser session. Prefer an existing headful Chrome session when it exposes DevTools through remote debugging: `agent-browser --auto-connect snapshot`, or `agent-browser --cdp 9222 snapshot` for an explicit port. A normal Chrome window without remote debugging cannot be attached; `agent-browser --headed` starts a new visible browser. Read a snapshot, act on an observed element, then take a fresh snapshot. Check appearance with a screenshot. Keep each tool's target IDs within that tool."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.cloud": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Use Cloud\n\nUse cmux Cloud when work needs a remote machine. List machines before choosing one. Use `exec` for a short command, `agent` for a coding agent, and `push` or `pull` for files. The default Cloud devbox includes Chrome and `cua-driver`; the Cloud guide explains how to verify and use them. Use the Cloud guide for desktop, browser, port, and machine lifecycle methods."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.computer": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Use app windows\n\nUse computer-use tools for native apps or visual controls. cmux provides these tools to Claude Code and Codex sessions launched in cmux. The first functional cmux Computer Use request opens setup automatically when needed. You can resume setup in Settings > cmux Computer Use. Read the `cmux-cua` skill for your agent's methods: inspect app or window state, click or type, then inspect again."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.intro": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "cmux puts terminals, browsers, and agents in one workspace. Use this guide to choose a method, then read its help before acting. `cmux --skill` prints this same guide."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.share": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Show work and find more methods\n\nUse Markdown and diff views to show results. Use notifications and progress to report task state. Read the Cloud guide for remote machines. Settings and integration docs list their own methods."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.targets": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Find your target\n\nA window contains workspaces. A workspace contains split panes. Each pane contains surfaces, such as a terminal or browser tab. Inspect the caller and use the returned IDs. Keep the caller's workspace and surface context; use explicit targets and `--focus false` where supported."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.overview.terminals": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "## Work with terminals\n\nCreate a workspace for a task, add panes for parallel work, and read terminal output before sending input. `send` enters text; `send-key` sends keys. Read the screen again to check the result."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.guide.usage": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage:"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agentInbox": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux agent inbox [--surface <target>] [--state queued|delivered|read|failed] [--limit <n>] [--mark-read] [--json]\n\nList agent messages, newest first. Without --surface, lists messages for\nevery surface. --mark-read marks the listed messages read."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agentMessage": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux agent message <target> [--from <name>] [--thread <id>] [--json] [--] <text|->\n       cmux agent message --reply-to <message-id> [--from <name>] [--json] [--] <text|->\n\nSend a message to the agent running in another workspace or surface. cmux\ndelivers it through that agent's hooks, never by typing into its terminal,\nso it cannot land in a half-typed prompt. An idle Claude Code session wakes\nup to read it; a busy one reads it at its next step.\n\n<target> is a workspace or surface id or ref (workspace:2, surface:5), or a\nworkspace title (exact, then a unique prefix). Use - to read the text from\nstdin. --from defaults to the sending workspace's title.\n\nExamples:\n  cmux agent message cmux-remote-status \"The relay fix is on main, rebase when free.\"\n  cmux agent message --reply-to 3f2a... \"Done, PR is #123.\""
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agentMessages": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux agent messages [on|off|status] [<target>] [--workspace] [--json]\n\nTurn agent messages off or on for one agent, or show whether it gets\nthem. Turning messages off for an agent refuses new messages to it and\nmarks the ones already queued for it as failed.\n\n<target> is resolved like cmux agent message: a workspace or surface id\nor ref, or a workspace title. Without one, the command applies to the\nsurface it runs in. --workspace applies to every surface in the\nworkspace instead.\n\nWithout --workspace, a workspace target means the one agent surface it\nresolves to now. That surface stays off even if a different surface\nbecomes the workspace's agent later.\n\nTo turn agent messages off everywhere, set agentMessages.enabled to\nfalse in ~/.config/cmux/cmux.json or turn off Settings > Automation >\nAgent Messages.\n\nExamples:\n  cmux agent messages off\n  cmux agent messages on workspace:3\n  cmux agent messages off --workspace cmux-remote-status"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agents.inbox": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "agent inbox [--surface <target>] [--state <state>] [--mark-read]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agents.message": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "agent message <target> [--from <name>] <text|->"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agents.messages": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "agent messages [on|off|status] [<target>] [--workspace]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.agents.reply": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "agent message --reply-to <id> [--from <name>] <text|->"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.clearNotifications": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux clear-notifications [--workspace <id|ref|index>] [--surface <id|ref|index>] [--window <id|ref|index>]\n\nClear queued notifications, optionally scoped to a workspace and surface.\n\nFlags: --workspace <id|ref|index> --surface <id|ref|index> --window <id|ref|index>"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.command.pr": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "pr <url|number> [flags] | pr clear [flags]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.description": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Show top-level CLI usage, or one task-focused command group."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.dismissNotification": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux dismiss-notification (--id <uuid> | --all-read)\n\nRemove one notification, or remove every already-read notification.\n\nFlags:\n  --id <uuid>           Notification id to remove\n  --all-read            Remove every already-read notification\n  --json                Print JSON\n  --id-format <mode>    refs, uuids, or both"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.fork": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "fork [--surface <id|ref>] <kind> <checkpoint-id> | fork --surface [id|ref]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ios": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "ios <subcommand> [args] [--surface <id|ref|index>]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.irohDiag": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux iroh-diag\n\nPrint the host's Iroh Connection Report as a plain-language timeline,\nthe same data as Settings > Networking > Connection Report."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.jumpToLastPrompt": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux jump-to-last-prompt\n\nFocus the surface where you last submitted a prompt to a coding agent, switching workspace and window as needed. With --json, opened is false when there is none.\n\nFlags:\n  --json                Print JSON\n  --id-format <mode>    refs, uuids, or both"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.jumpToUnread": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux jump-to-unread\n\nFocus the latest unread notification, matching the Notifications page action.\n\nFlags:\n  --json                Print JSON\n  --id-format <mode>    refs, uuids, or both"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.markNotificationRead": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux mark-notification-read (--id <uuid> | --workspace <id|ref> [--surface <id|ref>] | --all)\n\nMark notifications read without opening them. Exactly one selector is required.\n\nFlags:\n  --id <uuid>           Mark one notification read\n  --workspace <id|ref>  Mark notifications for a workspace\n  --surface <id|ref>    Narrow --workspace to one surface\n  --all                 Mark every notification read\n  --json                Print JSON\n  --id-format <mode>    refs, uuids, or both"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.memory": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux memory [flags]\n\nDiagnose cmux app memory separately from recursive terminal child-process RSS.\n\nFlags:\n  --all                         Include all windows (default: current window only)\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Limit workspace context for attribution labels\n  --groups <count>              Number of child command groups to show (default: 12)\n  --json                        Structured JSON output\n\nOutput:\n  App footprint is the direct cmux process physical footprint from macOS process accounting.\n  Child RSS is recursive resident memory for descendants of the cmux app process,\n  grouped by command name and attributed back to workspace, pane, and surface when known.\n\nExample:\n  cmux memory\n  cmux memory --groups 20\n  cmux --json memory --all"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.mosh": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux mosh <destination> [flags] [-- <remote-command-args>]\n\nMosh terminal transport:\n  --transport <ssh|mosh>  Interactive terminal transport (default: mosh)\n\nSSH continues to handle remote features; Mosh carries only the interactive\nterminal. If Mosh is missing locally or remotely, cmux reports it and uses SSH.\n\nExample:\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --transport mosh\n\n  cmux mosh dev@my-host"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.mosh-tmux": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux mosh-tmux <destination> [--session <name>] [flags]\n\nCreate a Mosh workspace that creates or attaches to a named tmux session (default: main). The tmux profile persists across reconnect and app session restore. This is a roaming tmux terminal, not the native mirror from cmux ssh-tmux. --session <name> selects the session; all other flags match cmux mosh. Without Mosh, the same profile runs over SSH.\n\nExample:\n  cmux mosh-tmux dev@my-host\n  cmux mosh-tmux dev@my-host --session agent-main"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.newWindow": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux new-window [--name <title>]\n\nCreate a new window.\n\nFlags:\n  --name <title>   Set the initial workspace and window title before the window appears.\n\nExamples:\n  cmux new-window\n  cmux new-window --name \"Build server\""
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.noSocket": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Also works without a running cmux app or socket."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.notify": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux notify [flags]\n\nSend a notification to a workspace/surface, or clear that resolved target with --clear.\n\nFlags:\n  --title <text>         Notification title (default: \"Notification\")\n  --subtitle <text>      Notification subtitle\n  --body <text>          Notification body\n  --reply                Allow a free-text inline reply\n  --desktop <true|false> Post a native macOS banner (default: true). false keeps the entry in the Notifications panel, sidebar badge and pane ring without a banner\n  --clear                Clear notifications for the resolved caller/target instead of posting\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace, except explicit surface UUIDs resolve globally\n  --surface <id|ref|index>     Target surface (refs/indexes use workspace/window context)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace/surface refs and indexes\n  --json                 Print the response payload as JSON\n  --id-format <mode>     refs, uuids, or both for human-readable ids\n\nThe response includes the created notification id. Use cmux dismiss-notification --id <uuid|notification:<uuid>>, cmux list-notifications, or cmux clear-notifications to manage notifications.\n--desktop sets the notification's desktop effect before notification hooks run; hooks can still override it. It has no effect with --clear.\n\nExample:\n  cmux notify --title \"Build done\" --body \"All tests passed\"\n  cmux notify --title \"Error\" --subtitle \"test.swift\" --body \"Line 42: syntax error\"\n  cmux notify --surface <uuid> --title \"Build done\"\n  cmux notify --title \"Deploy finished\" --desktop false\n  cmux notify --clear"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.notify.clear": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "--clear                Clear notifications for the resolved caller/target instead of posting"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.notify.management": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The response includes the created notification id. Use cmux dismiss-notification --id <uuid>, cmux list-notifications, or cmux clear-notifications to manage notifications."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.notify.reply": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "--reply                Allow a free-text inline reply"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.openFocusFlags": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Focus:\n  --focus                 Switch to the new workspace\n  --no-focus              Open it in the background"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.openNotification": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux open-notification --id <uuid>\n\nFocus the notification's workspace and surface, then mark the row read.\n\nFlags:\n  --id <uuid>           Notification id to open\n  --json                Print JSON\n  --id-format <mode>    refs, uuids, or both"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.paste": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux paste [flags] [--] [text | -]\n\nPaste text into a terminal surface through the same paste path as Cmd+V. If the running program has turned on bracketed paste, the text arrives as one paste and newlines stay inside it; otherwise each newline is sent as Enter. As with Cmd+V, control characters such as Esc, Backspace, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-U are replaced with spaces. Escape sequences such as \\n are not interpreted. With no text argument, or with - before --, the text is read from stdin. Text over 15 MiB after JSON escaping is rejected.\n\nIf an agent prompt in the target holds text someone is typing, or a question or permission dialog is open, nothing is sent and the command fails. --force sends anyway.\n\nFlags:\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace (default: $CMUX_WORKSPACE_ID)\n  --surface <id|ref|index>     Target surface (default: $CMUX_SURFACE_ID)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace/surface refs and indexes\n  --submit                     Press the agent's submit key after the paste\n  --force                      Send even over a draft or into an open dialog\n\nExample:\n  git diff | cmux paste --surface surface:2\n  cmux read-screen --surface surface:1 --lines 40 | cmux paste --surface surface:2\n  cmux paste --surface surface:2 --submit \"Review this change\""
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.readScreen": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux read-screen [flags]\n\nRead terminal text from a surface as plain text.\n\nFlags:\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace (default: $CMUX_WORKSPACE_ID)\n  --surface <id|ref|index>     Target surface (default: $CMUX_SURFACE_ID)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace/surface refs and indexes\n  --scrollback                 Include scrollback (not just visible viewport)\n  --lines <n>                  Limit to the last n lines (implies --scrollback)\n  --selection                  Read only the active selection; cannot be combined with --scrollback or --lines\n\nExample:\n  cmux read-screen\n  cmux read-screen --surface surface:2 --scrollback --lines 200\n  cmux read-screen --surface surface:2 --selection"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.readSelection": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux read-selection [flags]\n\nRead the active selection from any selectable surface. Plain output includes source context; --json returns the complete response.\n\nFlags:\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace (default: $CMUX_WORKSPACE_ID)\n  --surface <id|ref|index>     Target surface (default: $CMUX_SURFACE_ID)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace/surface refs and indexes\n\nExample:\n  cmux read-selection --surface surface:2\n  cmux read-selection --surface surface:2 --json"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.record": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux record [start] [flags]\n       cmux record stop|status [--id <id>]\n       cmux record note [--id <id>] <text>\n       cmux record list\n\nRecord a cmux window to an mp4 or gif, for a pull request or a bug\nreport. Only cmux's own windows can be recorded, and no Screen\nRecording permission is involved.\n\nFlags for start:\n  --format mp4|gif       Output format (default: mp4)\n  --gif                  Shorthand for --format gif\n  --fps <1-30>           Frames per second (default: 12 for mp4, 8 for gif)\n  --max-seconds <n>      Stop by itself after n seconds, 0.5-120 (default: 15)\n  --scale <0.1-1>        Scale the output (default: 1 for mp4, 0.5 for gif)\n  --max-width <64-4096>  Cap the output width, keeping the aspect ratio\n  --region <x,y,w,h>     Record part of the window, in window points\n  --out <path>           Write here instead of a temporary directory\n  --label <text>         Name the file\n  --no-captions          Do not draw `record note` captions into the clip\n  --window <id|ref|index>  Window to record (default: the frontmost one)\n\nOutput: `<id> <state> <frames> <path>`, or the full response with --json.\n\nA recording stops itself at --max-seconds, so a clip is never left\nrunning by an agent that goes away. One recording at a time.\n\nExample:\n  cmux record start --gif --max-seconds 8 --label sidebar-drag\n  cmux record note \"dragging the workspace\"\n  cmux record stop"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.reorderWorkspaces": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux reorder-workspaces --order <id|ref|index>,<id|ref|index>,... [flags]\n\nReorder workspaces within a window as one atomic batch. The comma-separated\norder is the final leading order inside the pinned and unpinned groups;\nunmentioned workspaces keep their relative order after listed peers in the\nsame group.\n\nFlags:\n  --order <refs>                Comma-separated workspace refs to place first\n  --window <id|ref|index>       Window context\n  --dry-run                     Print the resolved final indexes without applying\n\nExample:\n  cmux reorder-workspaces --order workspace:1,workspace:11,workspace:31\n  cmux reorder-workspaces --order workspace:11,workspace:1 --dry-run"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.restore": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "restore [--surface <id|ref>] <kind> <checkpoint-id> | restore --surface [id|ref]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.send": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux send [flags] [--] <text>\n\nSend text to a terminal surface as keystrokes. Escape sequences: \\n and \\r send Enter, \\t sends Tab.\n\nFor large text (for example over 4 KB, or several lines going to an agent), use --paste or cmux paste. Keystrokes press Enter at every newline, which can submit each line on its own.\n\nWith --paste, the text goes through the same paste path as cmux paste and Cmd+V, unchanged: escape sequences such as \\n are not interpreted, and control characters such as Esc and Ctrl-C are replaced with spaces. --paste must come before the text.\n\nIf an agent prompt in the target holds text someone is typing, or a question or permission dialog is open, nothing is sent and the command fails. --force sends anyway.\n\nFlags:\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace (default: $CMUX_WORKSPACE_ID)\n  --surface <id|ref|index>     Target surface (default: $CMUX_SURFACE_ID)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace/surface refs and indexes\n  --paste                      Paste the text instead of typing it\n  --force                      Send even over a draft or into an open dialog\n\nExample:\n  cmux send \"echo hello\"\n  cmux send --surface surface:2 \"ls -la\\n\"\n  cmux send --paste --surface surface:2 \"$(cat notes.md)\""
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.setStatus": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux set-status <key> <value> [flags]\n\nSet a sidebar status entry for a workspace. Status entries appear as\npills in the sidebar tab row. Use a unique key so different tools\n(e.g. \"claude_code\", \"build\") can manage their own entries.\n\nFlags:\n  --icon <name>          Icon name (e.g. \"sparkle\", \"hammer\")\n  --color <#hex>         Pill color (e.g. \"#ff9500\")\n                         \"#4C8DFF\" draws as the cmux accent (app.accentColor)\n  --priority <n>         Sort priority; higher appears first (default: 0)\n  --workspace <id|ref|index>   Target workspace (default: $CMUX_WORKSPACE_ID)\n  --window <id|ref|index>      Window context for workspace refs and indexes\n\nExample:\n  cmux set-status build \"compiling\" --icon hammer --color \"#ff9500\" --priority 80\n  cmux set-status deploy \"v1.2.3\" --workspace workspace:2"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.shot": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux shot [flags]\n\nScreenshot a cmux window, or a region of one, to a png or a jpeg. Only\ncmux's own windows can be captured, and no Screen Recording permission\nis involved, so this works on a release build and inside CI.\n\nFlags:\n  --format png|jpg       Output format (default: png)\n  --jpg                  Shorthand for --format jpg\n  --scale <0.1-1>        Scale the output (default: 1)\n  --max-width <64-8192>  Cap the output width, keeping the aspect ratio\n  --quality <0.1-1>      JPEG quality (default: 0.8, ignored for png)\n  --region <x,y,w,h>     Capture part of the window, in window points\n  --out <path>           Write here instead of a temporary directory\n  --label <text>         Name the file\n  --caption <text>       Draw a caption into the image\n  --window <id|ref|index>  Window to capture (default: the frontmost one)\n\nOutput: `<width>x<height> <bytes> <path>`, or the full response with --json.\n\nA region is the same rectangle `cmux record --region` films, so a\ndetail spotted in a clip can be shot with the same four numbers.\n\nExample:\n  cmux shot --label settings-sheet\n  cmux shot --region 0,0,420,900 --out ~/sidebar.png"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.simulator": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "simulator <subcommand> [args] [--surface <id|ref|index>]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux ssh <destination> [flags] [-- <remote-command-args>]\n\nCreate a new workspace, mark it as remote-SSH, and start an SSH session in that workspace.\ncmux will also establish a local SSH proxy endpoint so browser traffic can egress from the remote host.\n\nFlags:\n  --name <title>          Optional workspace title\n  --port <n>              SSH port\n  --identity <path>       SSH identity file path\n  -A, --forward-agent     Forward the caller's SSH agent; also honors ForwardAgent yes from ssh_config\n  -a, --no-forward-agent  Disable SSH agent forwarding for this workspace\n  --ssh-option <opt>      Extra SSH -o option (repeatable)\n  --window <id|ref|index> Target window for the managed workspace\n  --no-focus              Create workspace without switching to it\n\nExample:\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --name \"gpu-box\" --port 2222 --identity ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --forward-agent\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --ssh-option UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null --ssh-option StrictHostKeyChecking=no"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh-tmux": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Usage: cmux ssh-tmux <destination> [--port <n>] [--identity <path>] [--name <title>]\n                     [--transport ssh|et] [--transport-port <n>] [--broker <name>] [--no-focus]\n\nMirror a remote host's tmux sessions into the current window's sidebar over\nSSH tmux control mode (tmux -CC). Each session becomes a workspace, each\nwindow a tab, and each multi-pane window a native split. Requires the\n\"Remote tmux\" beta setting.\n\nIf the host needs interactive authentication (password, host-key confirmation,\nMFA, or a security-key touch), cmux runs ssh inline in this terminal so you can\nauthenticate, then mirrors the sessions over the shared SSH connection. Hosts\nthat authenticate non-interactively (ssh-agent / key in ~/.ssh/config) mirror\nwith no prompt. ~/.ssh/config aliases and their IdentityFile/ProxyJump/Port settings are honored.\n\nFlags:\n  --port <n>            SSH port\n  --identity <path>     SSH identity file path\n  --name <title>        Set the mirrored workspace's local display title. This is\n                        cosmetic only: it does not rename the remote tmux session.\n                        Applies to the first newly-mirrored session when the host\n                        has more than one.\n  --transport <name>    ssh (default) or et. et carries the control stream over EternalTerminal, which reconnects on its own after a network change\n  --transport-port <n>  Port the transport connects to: sshd's for ssh, etserver's for et (default 22 for ssh, 2022 for et)\n  --broker <name>       Reach the host through a broker declared under remoteTmux.brokers in cmux.json (a named entry, not a command)\n  --no-focus            Do not select the mirror workspace or focus its window\n\nExample:\n  cmux ssh-tmux dev@my-host\n  cmux ssh-tmux dev@my-host --port 2222 --identity ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n  cmux ssh-tmux dev@my-host --name \"prod db\"\n  cmux ssh-tmux dev@my-host --transport et --transport-port 8080\n  cmux ssh-tmux my-host --transport et --broker corp"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh-tmux.helperPath": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "--transport-helper-path <path>  Absolute path to etterminal on the remote host (direct ET only). Omit to use ET's PATH lookup; a broker supplies its own helper path."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh-tmux.newWindow": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Additional flag:\n  --new-window        Open the mirror in a dedicated new window"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh.initialCommand": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Initial command:\n  --command <text>        Run text once in the initial remote terminal after shell startup\n\nExample:\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --command 'omp \"investigate auth\"'"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "cli.help.ssh.mosh": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Mosh terminal transport:\n  --transport <ssh|mosh>  Interactive terminal transport (default: ssh)\n\nSSH continues to handle remote features; Mosh carries only the interactive\nterminal. If Mosh is missing locally or remotely, cmux reports it and uses SSH.\n\nExample:\n  cmux ssh dev@my-host --transport mosh"
          }
        }
      }
    },
